erpretation:** A P/E of 20 means investors pay $20 for every $1 of annual earnings. Higher P/Es usually signal that investors expect faster future growth or see lower risk. Lower P/Es can signal value, or that the market expects weak growth or sees more risk.\n- **Trailing vs. forward:** Trailing P/E uses the last 12 months of earnings. Forward P/E uses estimated earnings for the next 12 months.\n- **Earnings yield:** The inverse (EPS ÷ price) gives the earnings yield, which you can compare with bond yields.\n- **Limits:** P/E is hard to compare across industries with very different growth and capital needs. It is distorted when earnings are negative, unusually high, or one-off. It says nothing about debt, and it is most useful alongside other metrics such as growth rate, free cash flow, and debt levels."}]},"stop_reason":"end_turn","usage":{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":353,"cache_read_input_tokens":4517,"cache_creation_input_tokens":90},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","refusal_category":null}]}
